#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444153} :activity/id #uuid "9a145625-7556-5712-927a-f0c028bebc85" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048395 :topic/id #uuid "9bb55214-36f6-5124-91d2-24ff1cb653aa" :topic/math-academy-id 3242} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444150 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305096 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2af9e8ee-427e-5b4a-9c20-64d8c764d689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444123} {:answer/id #uuid "4cfdedb2-5206-5bb7-bc0c-b4380ef84ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444126} {:answer/id #uuid "6228cec8-4f90-5557-9758-248e08a02159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444128} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7adf14-40d3-5a8b-b017-a61006c8a570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444135} {:answer/id #uuid "e2fef36d-c8ab-5030-a6ef-407316ba8a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444128} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7c351da-96ee-57a7-ad19-0d1fa7a32b4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "05eed77e-6ddf-5b27-b62c-35f5a73a8482" :question/math-academy-id "q-332692" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aaeba63f6237121fe226eb30e5378d3e93129bb2782b9c8acb8d73ac719676b3.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0f0eb2d5-220a-51b8-8ecc-e34f70d2ec6c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444151}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6095321f-3c3a-5564-bd76-8ac789465110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/63/635e8687fbccc7612f692ec1a1abaa9802bafe9c3773e4b0c058a18813e7249e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391146} {:answer/id #uuid "9f24dea2-7b89-5ce9-93de-ca7b97049c38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a5/a5ae215022638430d0c89dddd97c01ae7598b6251bd013ef48ca9771029b8cd6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391147} {:answer/id #uuid "f70a8ce1-5b6e-5473-90f3-bf87bae67685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c9/c9816edf7fb99b759361abc4333f3ed0d3676d2c77db2210367466b66837bca8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391148} {:answer/id #uuid "9fcceae6-4fa4-5965-9386-71fc659c0936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c4/c497d1f9dd6cb5bc473bd46ec56c465228408447fb2436b80f16a23b282654b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391149} {:answer/id #uuid "65324022-a078-5bdb-b4aa-e0381dbb7e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4f/4f613e414cdb619a9b09f25a47b7e4cad953440c40d91f876322d7e1cb59b8c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391150}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391149} :answer-field/id #uuid "41bd2e0d-7517-5053-bcbd-08e7df5307b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "34a4a204-b6f5-559d-8b71-d224e42d358b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333177" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=9x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{9t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{9t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{9t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/99/99d6e9894c7117c5684e17702540d0eb469b6e824381cf2263c843f8f55ee8d8.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{9t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{9t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{9{c}_{1}{e}^{9t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{9{c}_{1}}{e}^{-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/c4/c497d1f9dd6cb5bc473bd46ec56c465228408447fb2436b80f16a23b282654b2.png)"} :step/id #uuid "54129fc6-c6e7-58f1-988d-b38903f3b41b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444154}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59d44033-2953-53f5-9485-d986fa666d6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444127} {:answer/id #uuid "73a7ffd1-1cf0-5378-bc11-62171a0c3abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444131}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444131} :answer-field/id #uuid "62075cea-c86f-5ede-af51-3b9b6e1b7a2a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444143} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a24a7424-a635-59c1-b0a8-3d8d09c20d84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444134} {:answer/id #uuid "d97d6664-8e4c-536c-b723-d007c6be0edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444138} :answer-field/id #uuid "b02dcf68-1aa6-50e4-bcf6-a43a1c61f609" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444144} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d7975e3-2735-589b-a5cb-e7baa70e5b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444120} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8766a2-b885-5372-9ea9-597fde6b723c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444121} {:answer/id #uuid "40931ed8-a2d3-5a7d-af42-4ceeceafa5f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444125}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444120} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7391bdf-0a46-510a-b0ad-0b46b06955a6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2cc95b14-28b1-5770-bd6e-4ea958da90bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-333222" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/66/665ff96d191a9b2dc6dc57f1c3a97743dcb1a26c4118151716bdf430a4c014f3.png)"} :step/id #uuid "aed772c0-58fb-5fcf-9ea0-9de0620f174d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40549b82-d47f-561c-8931-d7d35ef461cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444124} {:answer/id #uuid "62f8b741-a9bc-5f56-8242-c18d258a8e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444130} {:answer/id #uuid "85b1285c-0fbc-5e41-b4e0-700ef6b3681e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444132} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc563a4-4937-522e-9fb1-2f13c18af602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444133} {:answer/id #uuid "ed7d8b7f-1e93-5e81-b22e-1d876ddc07f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444141}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444133} :answer-field/id #uuid "540f62cb-7d1e-5455-b752-8d1fe82b863d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4e106ec5-d244-5095-9a3e-86feed9bed83" :question/math-academy-id "q-332687" :question/problem "![](images/39/390321da0e4c3367d121a1561ccc41ca4960c912ff53d909acd1c90f505758ce.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c9e90e37-14ef-58a0-a386-45d0aaa27b85" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444150}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24461d44-24c5-575d-be64-3d0e46a32765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/7a/7aaaf92ef853dcc88c787120fbb380a1529a49504255fb1b05803e3712587c90.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444122} {:answer/id #uuid "62879d01-d1d9-55e2-ab3d-537c10ca44a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/19/196d1a61793b619bf86f3ed18852f819cd27821e0ad0388bcd05621e60ba46ba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444129} {:answer/id #uuid "c46723d7-b8d3-5ade-85f9-0bf37a1a2dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f1/f1162ad9109f4117ea94321ee1e2192734b3a895b35232a0788c0ff754e2bade.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444136} {:answer/id #uuid "ce200fd8-672b-5314-9ac6-4d8b99ea280c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8c/8c350c21a6206ec73244dd58601b20a816dcb69557a030099870e2c824aba772.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444137} {:answer/id #uuid "dd125c4d-09c0-5767-a865-91e186cba8d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9a/9ab329eed7e67e017a822e8d5163b331e0c93a9747f74aee3a10d6592f44a59b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444139}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444139} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfc6238d-540c-5d03-81af-c08e27ebdfce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "97c6c7fa-18db-5e5d-84d1-3076bd6f5c92" :question/math-academy-id "q-333183" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/a2/a2c81ff3c50013c149dc6a5db67bbe11cccdbbef22ce2c0712101f4a358bcb8c.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{2t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{2t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{t})^{′}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}}{{c}_{1}{e}^{t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{{c}_{1}}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/9a/9ab329eed7e67e017a822e8d5163b331e0c93a9747f74aee3a10d6592f44a59b.png)"} :step/id #uuid "dbe1ae80-d585-551e-b83d-d3f42e88a7bb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444152}}] :activity/title "Phase Portraits for Decoupled Linear Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444119}]}
